# What is Networking


## Goal
   Intro about the notion of Networking and how it applies in our modern world


## Summary
   A Networking is two or more systems echanging informations (like devices connected).
   The Internet is one giant network that consists of many small networks within itself. I was created by Tim Berners-Lee in 1989 as World Wide Web (www.).
   This small networks are called private networks, and private networks are connected by public networks (aka the Internet).

   There are 2 ways to identify a device : through it's IP adress and it's MAC adress.

   IP adress, short for Internet Protocol (the name of the device). 
      Each device has 2 IP, one for public networks and one for private networks. 
      Public IP adresses are given by our Internet Service Provider (ISP), and used to identify the device on the internet.
      Private adress is used to be identify amongst other devices.
      IP adresses are devided into four octets.
      The number calculated through the technique IP "adressing & subnetting".
      Can change from device to device.
      Cannot be active simultaneously on the same network.
      First version of the Internet Protocol IPv4, using the numbering system 2^32 IP adresses (~ 4.29 billion).
      Second version of the Internet Protocol IPv6, 2^128 IP adresses (340 trillion-plus) and more efficient.
         ex : IPv4 : 86.157.52.21
              IPv6 : 2001:0db8:85a3:0000:0000:8a2e:0370:7334
      IPv1-3 and 5 stayed experimentals 

   MAC adress, short for Media Access Control adress (the fingerprint of the device, similar to a serial number).
      Devices on a network will all have a physical network interface, a microchip board found on the device's motherboard.
      This interface is assigned a unique adress MAC.
      Twelve characters hexadecimal (based sixteen). The six first characters represent the company that made the network, six last are unique.
         ex : a4:c3:f0:85:ac:2d
      Used as a "physical identifier for a device on a network".

   Firewall is a security toll, hardware or software, used to filter unauthorized traffic in the network

   Ping uses ICMP (Internet Control Message Protocol)

   LAN (Local Area Network) Topologies
      Star Topology :
         All devices connected direclty to a switch or a hub. Packets are sent through the switch/hub to be distributed to the destination device.
         Cost a lot, but reliable and scalable. Tho if the switch/hub fail, the Network will be down.
      Bus Topology : 
         All devices connected directly to the backbone cable (one main cable). If the cable breaks, the network fails.
         All data are sent on both side of the cable (devices before AND devices after in the backbone order) so very easy to slow and bottleneck.
         Easy and cost-efficient topology
      Ring / Token Topology :
         All devices connected in loop. When data is sent, it goes through all the devices until the destinated one.
         If a device receive a data that is not for him AND have a data to send himself, the device will first sent its data THEN the one it has to send.

   A switch reunite multiple devices in one cable and transmite data. Un switch connecte un appareil à un réseau (le couloir d'un bâtiment).
      Utilise les adresses MAC
      Il retient quel appareil est connecté à quel port
   A router connects networks and pass data. Un router connecte plusieurs réseaux entre eux (le carrefour qui relie plusieurs bâtiments).
      Utilise les adresses IP
      Indispensable pour accéder à Internet ou relier deux LAN.

   Subnetting is dividing a Network into smaller networks (for example for different departments like accounting, finance and HR). It provides Efficiency, Security and Full Control.
   Subnets use IP addresses in three different ways
      Network Address : to identify the start of a network
      Host Address : to identify devices within a network
      Default Gateway : to identify the device responsible for sending data to another network.  

   ARP (= Address Resolution Protocol) is the technology tasked to found the IP address of a known MAC adress.
      ARP Request : It'll send a message on the Network asking "Guys, I have this MAC address, who is it ? Can I have it's IP address ? Thanks. Cheers"
      ARP Reply : And it'll receive the answer "Hey, yess it's me, lemme send you my IP address : []. See ya !"
      Once the ARP is informed of an identified IP address corresponding to a MAC address, it stores it on its cache.

   DHCP (= Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol) assign automatically an IP address to a device connecting to a network. 
      DHCP Discover : When a device connecting to a network, sends a request to have an IP address "Hey guys, who can give me an IP please ? Cheers"
      DHCP Offer : The DHCP answer the call and assign an IP address "Hey dude, here's an IP address, sounds good to you ?"
      DHCP Request : Device send a confirmation to the DHCP "Looks good to me ! Thanks mate"
      DHCP ACK : Last reply acknowledging the information "Great ! I take note, cheers !"




   
